.TH LOADAVG 1 "2026-08-29" "dots" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
loadavg \- one-line load average for the tmux status bar
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B loadavg
.SH DESCRIPTION
Prints the 1, 5 and 15-minute load averages on a single line,
space-separated, with no decoration. It exists for
.BR tmux (1)'s
status bar: the inline
.I fallback
status bar in
.I .tmux.conf
invokes it as
.I "#($HOME/bin/loadavg)"
and supplies all color, spacing and labels itself, while this script
supplies only the three numbers. The primary status bar is the
tmux-powerline plugin, whose built-in
.I load
segment renders the load average when the plugin is installed; the
fallback bar (and this shim) takes over only when the clone is missing.
.PP
It takes no options and reads no input; a status-line refresh simply runs it
once per interval.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc/loadavg
Primary source on Linux, including WSL2 and OrbStack: the first three
fields are printed verbatim.
.TP
.I "sysctl -n vm.loadavg"
Used everywhere else (macOS, FreeBSD). Darwin and BSD wrap the value in
braces (like
.IR "( 1.00 0.50 0.25 )")
so the awk program prints fields 2 through 4.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. Any shell error (missing
.BR awk ,
neither
.I /proc/loadavg
nor
.BR sysctl (8)
available) leaves the status segment empty for that refresh; with
.I set -u
in effect an unexpected unset variable aborts with status 2.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux\-copy (1),
.BR dots\-setup (1)